  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Skies are mostly sunny out there across\nthe entire forecast area. There are a few mid clouds hanging\naround central Nevada but not enough to block out the plentiful\nsunshine. \n\nNorthern and central Nevada is sitting west of an upper ridge in\nnorthern Utah. This feature will slide to the east and north\nduring the overnight. The weather will remain quiet tonight with\nlows in the 30s and 40s.\n\nAn upper level trough will be diving to the south and east Friday.\nSurface winds will be on the increase from the southwest with\nspeeds gusting from 30 to 40 mph. A few locales could see gusts to\n45 mph but effects will remain localized. Patchy blowing dust is\npossible during the afternoon that could drop the visibility to\nthree miles or so. Afternoon high temperatures will be in the 70s\nand 80s. Look for the winds to diminish during the early to mid\nportions of the evening. Overnight lows temperatures will be in \nthe 30s and 40s.\n\nThe main upper low will remain well north of Nevada for Saturday.\nSouthwest flow will continue across the area which means dry\nconditions will persist. Winds are forecast to be strong again\nacross central nevada with gusts of 25 to 35 mph. High\ntemperatures will cool several degrees with readings in the 70s\nand 80s once again. No precipitation and limited cloudiness is\nexpected for Saturday night into Sunday with lows in the 30s and\n40s.\n\nAnother wave is expected to approach the western United States\nSunday. This one is further south than the previous trough and\nwill be moving on shore in California during the afternoon. Skies\nwill become partly to mostly cloudy during the day with the\npossibility of isolated rain showers during the late afternoon \nand overnight across the forecast area. Amounts will be light with\nmost valley locales seeing nothing up to five hundredths of an \ninch. Highs will again cool by a degree or so with readings\ngenerally in the 70s. Overnight lows will be in the 40s. \n\nThe upper low will continue its eastward progression Monday and\nTuesday. The deepest moisture is expected to remain far east and\nsouth of the forecast area with scattered rain showers clipping\nthe eastern and east-central portions of the forecast area,\nincluding White Pine county. Highs both afternoons will be in the\n70s with lows in the 30s and 40s. \n\nThe flow remains westerly for Wednesday and Thursday, which means\nmainly dry conditions. Highs are expected to warm a few degrees\neach day with readings in the 70s and 80s. Lows will be in the\n30s and 40s.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Only minor adjustments were made\nto the wind and wind gust grids for Friday and Saturday afternoon.\nOtherwise, no other changes were made to the NBM. \n\nConfidence is high for the strong winds Friday and Saturday\nafternoon, along with slight cooling each day. Meanwhile,\nconfidence in the light rain showers increases to moderate for\nSunday, though placement of activity and amounts remain low. This\nincludes the activity seen Monday as well. There is moderate to \nhigh confidence in the return of upper level ridging for the \nmiddle of next week restoring quiet and dry pattern over Nevada.",
